/**
 * Brand marks for the connector catalog.
 *
 * Vendored so nothing is fetched at runtime and no icon dependency reaches the
 * bundle, from three sources in order of preference: `@thesvg/icons` (MIT,
 * https://thesvg.org), `simple-icons` (CC0, https://simpleicons.org), and, for
 * brands in neither set, the vendor's own site -- the SVG their header or
 * favicon actually serves. A brand with no vector logo at all lives in
 * `rasterMarks` instead. Marks remain their owners' trademarks; they are used
 * only to identify the service they belong to.
 *
 * Three rules are worth knowing before adding one, all learned by rendering the
 * result rather than reading the file:
 *
 * - `mono` follows what the source ships, not "is it one colour". A mark that
 *   arrives with a brand colour KEEPS it (asana, linear, grafana, semgrep); only
 *   marks that arrive with no colour, or a purely neutral one, are drawn in
 *   currentColor. And a knockout mark -- a dark ground with the glyph cut out of
 *   it, like Guru or Runway -- must stay `mono: false` whatever its colour count,
 *   because stripping the fills collapses it into a solid blob.
 *
 * - A brand with no icon-only mark gets NO entry. Unstructured publishes only a
 *   wordmark, which renders as an unreadable sliver in a 40px tile;
 *   ConnectionIcon's tinted monogram reads better and is the intended fallback.
 *
 * - Do NOT "optimise" this file by rounding path coordinates. It was tried: at
 *   two decimals Fal disappeared outright and Buildkite and Jam distorted,
 *   because their viewBoxes are small enough that two decimals is coarser than
 *   the artwork. It saved 6.9 kB raw, about 1 kB gzipped, for broken logos.
 *
 * `mono` marks are drawn in `currentColor`, so they take the tile's neutral
 * foreground and stay legible in both themes — that is what keeps a near-black
 * mark like Notion, GitHub or Vercel visible on a dark background. The rest
 * carry their own palette.
 *
 * `markup` is the inner SVG of a build-time constant, never user input.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * To add a brand, take its SVG from thesvg.org and apply four steps by hand:
 *
 * 1. Pick the right image. Several brands publish a wordmark as their default —
 *    Stripe and monday.com both do — and a company name renders as an
 *    unreadable sliver in a 40px tile. Use the icon, not the wordmark.
 *
 * 2. Drop the outer <svg> tag and keep only what is inside it. Its hardcoded
 *    width/height would override the size ConnectionIcon asks for.
 *
 * 3. Delete any <style>, <metadata>, <title> or <desc>. A <style> block is the
 *    one that bites: inlined into the page its class names are global, so a
 *    `.st0 { fill: ... }` rule would repaint anything else using that class.
 *
 * 4. Prefix every id with the slug, and every reference to it — `id="a"`,
 *    `url(#a)`, `href="#a"` all become `atlassian-a`. Ids are document-global
 *    once inlined and the published marks DO collide: Atlassian, Google and
 *    Neon each ship an `id="a"`. Left alone, whichever renders first wins the
 *    reference and Atlassian draws itself with Google's gradient.
 *
 * Then set `mono: true` if the mark is a single colour, and strip its fill so
 * it inherits `currentColor`; otherwise `mono: false` and leave its fills be.
 */
export interface BrandMark {
  viewBox: string
  markup: string
  /** True when the mark is drawn in `currentColor` and takes the tile's colour. */
  mono: boolean
}

